      <section class="tab-section" id="tab-manual" data-title="Manual">
        <div class="manual-guide-shell">
          <div>
            <h2>Manual</h2>
            <p class="description">
              Manual Mode is the direct configuration workflow. Use this guide section when you want to understand what each Manual tab, field, and action button means before creating or testing a run.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="manual-guide-controls">
            <label class="manual-guide-label" for="manual-guide-select">
              Manual guide tab
              <span>Choose the Manual page tab you want explained.</span>
              <select class="manual-guide-select" id="manual-guide-select">
                <option value="run_identity">0 — Run identity</option>
                <option value="pipeline">1 — Pipeline</option>
                <option value="slurm_general">2 — Slurm general</option>
                <option value="stage_resources">3 — Stage resources</option>
                <option value="data_catalogs">4 — Data catalogs</option>
                <option value="required_inputs">5 — Required inputs</option>
                <option value="optional_inputs">6 — Optional inputs</option>
                <option value="model_settings">7 — Model settings</option>
                <option value="elevation_mask">8 — Elevation / mask</option>
                <option value="roughness">9 — Roughness</option>
                <option value="subgrid">10 — Subgrid</option>
                <option value="forcing">11 — Forcing</option>
                <option value="infiltration">12 — Infiltration</option>
                <option value="output">13 — Output</option>
                <option value="advanced">14 — Advanced</option>
                <option value="postprocess">15 — Postprocess</option>
                <option value="safety_debug">16 — Safety / debug</option>
                <option value="backend_paths">17 — Backend paths</option>
                <option value="check_all">Check all</option>
                <option value="submit">Submit</option>
              </select>
            </label>

            <p class="manual-guide-hint">
              This section mirrors the real Manual Mode tabs. Each guide tab explains what that part of the page is for, what the controls mean, when to change them, and what mistakes to avoid.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="manual-guide-panel" data-manual-guide-panel="run_identity">
            <div class="manual-guide-card">
              <h3>0 — Run identity</h3>
              <p>
                This tab names the run and decides where the run folder will be created. These fields matter because the backend uses them to write configs, scripts, logs, model inputs, SFINCS outputs, and postprocessing products.
              </p>
              <div class="manual-field-table-wrap">
                <table class="manual-field-table">
                  <thead>
                    <tr>
                      <th>Field / button</th>
                      <th>What it means</th>
                      <th>What to watch for</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody>
                    <tr>
                      <td><span class="manual-inline-code">run_name</span></td>
                      <td>The folder name for this run under the output root.</td>
                      <td>Use a unique name. Reusing a finished run name can make Build Scripts or Submit fail, which is intentional safety behavior.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td><span class="manual-inline-code">output_root</span></td>
                      <td>The parent folder where run folders are written.</td>
                      <td>For shared project runs, this is usually the shared SFINCS runs folder. Be careful with overwrite settings.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td><span class="manual-inline-code">project_root</span></td>
                      <td>The pipeline project folder used by the backend.</td>
                      <td>Most users should leave this at the current shared pipeline root unless deliberately testing another bundle.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Browse buttons</td>
                      <td>Open the launcher path browser for path fields.</td>
                      <td>The browser only allows safe roots. A path can look valid but still be rejected if outside allowed roots.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>New run / wipe page</td>
                      <td>Resets the page fields back toward defaults.</td>
                      <td>Use before starting a fresh setup. Save anything important first.</td>
                    </tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="manual-callout">
              <p>
                <strong>Key idea:</strong> Preflight can validate an imported config even if the destination run folder already exists. Build Scripts and Submit should block real existing run folders unless overwrite behavior is intentionally enabled.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="manual-guide-panel" data-manual-guide-panel="pipeline">
            <div class="manual-guide-card">
              <h3>1 — Pipeline</h3>
              <p>
                This tab describes the workflow mode behind the page. Manual Mode should stay the HydroMT-build/manual-config workflow. Native file override behavior belongs in Override Mode, not here.
              </p>
              <div class="manual-field-table-wrap">
                <table class="manual-field-table">
                  <thead>
                    <tr>
                      <th>Setting</th>
                      <th>Manual meaning</th>
                      <th>Warning</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody>
                    <tr>
                      <td><span class="manual-inline-code">preprocess_mode</span></td>
                      <td>Manual should force <span class="manual-inline-code">hydromt_build</span>.</td>
                      <td>Do not let a loaded Override config silently change this to hybrid or native assembly.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td><span class="manual-inline-code">pipeline_mode</span></td>
                      <td>The backend action is chosen by the Run Preflight, Build Scripts, or Submit buttons.</td>
                      <td>This should not be a confusing visible choice on the normal Manual setup tabs.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td><span class="manual-inline-code">use_sfincs_file_overrides</span></td>
                      <td>Manual should force this false.</td>
                      <td>Native SFINCS overrides belong in Override Mode.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Use current pipeline bundle runtime paths</td>
                      <td>Prefer the current launcher/pipeline scripts, env, and container paths over stale loaded-config paths.</td>
                      <td>Normal users should leave this enabled.</td>
                    </tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="manual-guide-panel" data-manual-guide-panel="slurm_general">
            <div class="manual-guide-card">
              <h3>2 — Slurm general</h3>
              <p>
                This tab controls general HPC job behavior. These settings do not change the physical model directly, but they determine whether the jobs have enough time, memory, and scheduler resources to run.
              </p>
              <div class="manual-field-table-wrap">
                <table class="manual-field-table">
                  <thead>
                    <tr>
                      <th>Control</th>
                      <th>What it does</th>
                      <th>Safe habit</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Slurm account / partition</td>
                      <td>Chooses where the jobs are submitted on the cluster.</td>
                      <td>Use the project-approved account/partition for shared runs.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Job time limits</td>
                      <td>Sets maximum wall time before Slurm kills a stage.</td>
                      <td>Use short times for tests and longer times for full events.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Memory requests</td>
                      <td>Requests RAM for each stage.</td>
                      <td>Postprocessing can need more memory than expected for large maps/quicklooks.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Submit behavior</td>
                      <td>Controls whether scripts are only built or actually submitted.</td>
                      <td>Treat preflight, build, and submit as separate decisions.</td>
                    </tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="manual-guide-panel" data-manual-guide-panel="stage_resources">
            <div class="manual-guide-card">
              <h3>3 — Stage resources</h3>
              <p>
                The pipeline has separate stages: preprocessing, SFINCS solver, and postprocessing. This tab controls resources for each stage separately.
              </p>
              <div class="manual-field-table-wrap">
                <table class="manual-field-table">
                  <thead>
                    <tr>
                      <th>Stage</th>
                      <th>What it does</th>
                      <th>Resource note</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Preprocess</td>
                      <td>Builds or assembles the SFINCS model inputs.</td>
                      <td>Usually not CPU-heavy unless subgrid or large data processing is active.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>SFINCS</td>
                      <td>Runs the actual flood model solver.</td>
                      <td>CPU count matters here. More CPUs should be justified by utilization and wall-time improvement.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Postprocess</td>
                      <td>Creates maps, hydrographs, summaries, and quicklooks.</td>
                      <td>Large outputs can make this stage memory-heavy.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>OpenMP / threads</td>
                      <td>Controls solver threading behavior.</td>
                      <td>Match requested CPUs with solver threading settings so CPUs are actually used.</td>
                    </tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="manual-guide-panel" data-manual-guide-panel="data_catalogs">
            <div class="manual-guide-card">
              <h3>4 — Data catalogs</h3>
              <p>
                Data catalogs tell HydroMT and the backend where to find source datasets or curated event inputs. In Manual Mode, this is one of the most important setup tabs.
              </p>
              <div class="manual-field-table-wrap">
                <table class="manual-field-table">
                  <thead>
                    <tr>
                      <th>Field / button</th>
                      <th>What it means</th>
                      <th>Common mistake</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody>
                    <tr>
                      <td><span class="manual-inline-code">data_catalogs</span></td>
                      <td>A list of catalog paths used by the backend.</td>
                      <td>It may be stored as a JSON list. That is normal.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Browse</td>
                      <td>Selects catalog folders or files through the path browser.</td>
                      <td>Do not paste a random local Windows path; the backend needs Longleaf-visible paths.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Runtime-window review</td>
                      <td>Compares requested model times to the selected event catalog runtime metadata.</td>
                      <td>This catches catalog-vs-model time mismatch, but it does not yet inspect every forcing file timestamp.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Catalog order</td>
                      <td>Controls which catalogs are available to HydroMT/backend resolution.</td>
                      <td>Use curated/current catalogs, not stale test catalogs, unless deliberately testing.</td>
                    </tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="manual-callout">
              <p>
                <strong>Important:</strong> the catalog runtime check is not the same as full forcing-file coverage validation. Full rainfall/BZS/DIS timestamp coverage checks are a later deeper validation layer.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="manual-guide-panel" data-manual-guide-panel="required_inputs">
            <div class="manual-guide-card">
              <h3>5 — Required inputs</h3>
              <p>
                This tab covers inputs that a HydroMT-build run generally needs before the model can be created, such as the region, elevation/topobathy source, and other base data.
              </p>
              <div class="manual-field-table-wrap">
                <table class="manual-field-table">
                  <thead>
                    <tr>
                      <th>Input</th>
                      <th>What it controls</th>
                      <th>Warning</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Region path or bounding box</td>
                      <td>Defines the physical model area.</td>
                      <td>A missing or wrong region means the whole model domain is wrong.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>DEM / topobathy source</td>
                      <td>Provides elevations/depths for the model grid.</td>
                      <td>Vertical datum consistency matters. Do not mix sources blindly.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>CRS / projection</td>
                      <td>Defines how coordinates are interpreted.</td>
                      <td>Wrong CRS can shift the model away from the real location.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Required browse buttons</td>
                      <td>Help select paths from allowed roots.</td>
                      <td>Blank paths may be okay only when the selected mode does not need that input.</td>
                    </tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="manual-guide-panel" data-manual-guide-panel="optional_inputs">
            <div class="manual-guide-card">
              <h3>6 — Optional inputs</h3>
              <p>
                Optional inputs add model detail, validation outputs, or extra physical features. They should be enabled only when the needed source data is available and the backend supports that feature.
              </p>
              <div class="manual-field-table-wrap">
                <table class="manual-field-table">
                  <thead>
                    <tr>
                      <th>Optional group</th>
                      <th>Purpose</th>
                      <th>Safe habit</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Observation points / lines</td>
                      <td>Requests history output at gauges or cross sections.</td>
                      <td>Enable only when the obs path is valid.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Structures</td>
                      <td>Adds thin dams, weirs, drainage structures, culverts, or similar controls.</td>
                      <td>Do not enable a structure family without a valid source/path and backend support.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Land cover / reclass tables</td>
                      <td>Supports roughness or infiltration mapping.</td>
                      <td>A blank reclass table may be a warning depending on selected method.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Validation layers</td>
                      <td>Help compare outputs to gauges, HWMs, or other validation data.</td>
                      <td>Validation setup should not be confused with model forcing.</td>
                    </tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="manual-guide-panel" data-manual-guide-panel="model_settings">
            <div class="manual-guide-card">
              <h3>7 — Model settings</h3>
              <p>
                Model settings control the model clock, output cadence, grid spacing, CRS behavior, and other SFINCS settings that affect the run itself.
              </p>
              <div class="manual-field-table-wrap">
                <table class="manual-field-table">
                  <thead>
                    <tr>
                      <th>Setting</th>
                      <th>What it means</th>
                      <th>Warning</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody>
                    <tr>
                      <td><span class="manual-inline-code">tref</span>, <span class="manual-inline-code">tstart</span>, <span class="manual-inline-code">tstop</span></td>
                      <td>Reference time, model start time, and model stop time.</td>
                      <td>These must match the forcing/event window closely enough for the run to make sense.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Output intervals</td>
                      <td>How often map/history/restart outputs are written.</td>
                      <td>Too-frequent output can create large files and slow postprocessing.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Grid spacing</td>
                      <td>Controls model resolution.</td>
                      <td>Finer grids are more expensive and need more careful input data.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>CRS / geographic flags</td>
                      <td>Controls projected-vs-geographic coordinate interpretation.</td>
                      <td>For Harris County-style projected runs, safe defaults should be checked carefully.</td>
                    </tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="manual-guide-panel" data-manual-guide-panel="elevation_mask">
            <div class="manual-guide-card">
              <h3>8 — Elevation / mask</h3>
              <p>
                This tab controls the elevation/depth surface and which cells are active, inactive, or boundary cells. Mistakes here can break the model geometry before forcing is even considered.
              </p>
              <div class="manual-field-table-wrap">
                <table class="manual-field-table">
                  <thead>
                    <tr>
                      <th>Control</th>
                      <th>What it affects</th>
                      <th>Warning</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody>
                    <tr>
                      <td>DEM / bathy paths</td>
                      <td>Elevation and bathymetry used to build depths.</td>
                      <td>Check datum and units before mixing sources.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Mask settings</td>
                      <td>Active, inactive, boundary, and source-relevant grid cells.</td>
                      <td>Wrong masks can remove important flooded areas or create invalid boundaries.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Region mode</td>
                      <td>Whether the model is built from a geometry path or bounding box.</td>
                      <td>Blank geometry path with geometry mode selected should fail checks.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Elevation-related browse buttons</td>
                      <td>Select topobathy or region inputs.</td>
                      <td>Use backend-visible paths, not local laptop paths.</td>
                    </tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="manual-guide-panel" data-manual-guide-panel="roughness">
            <div class="manual-guide-card">
              <h3>9 — Roughness</h3>
              <p>
                Roughness controls flow resistance. Manual Mode may support uniform roughness, landcover-derived roughness, or catalog-driven roughness depending on the selected setup.
              </p>
              <div class="manual-field-table-wrap">
                <table class="manual-field-table">
                  <thead>
                    <tr>
                      <th>Control</th>
                      <th>What it does</th>
                      <th>Warning</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Uniform Manning value</td>
                      <td>Applies a single roughness value broadly.</td>
                      <td>Simple but less spatially detailed.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Landcover roughness</td>
                      <td>Maps landcover classes to roughness values.</td>
                      <td>Needs valid landcover source and reclass table/config.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>HydroMT roughness sources</td>
                      <td>Uses catalog/source definitions for roughness setup.</td>
                      <td>Blank source config should warn when this method is enabled.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Native roughness files</td>
                      <td>Prebuilt files such as <span class="manual-inline-code">sfincs.manning</span>.</td>
                      <td>Native file workflows belong mainly in Override Mode.</td>
                    </tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="manual-guide-panel" data-manual-guide-panel="subgrid">
            <div class="manual-guide-card">
              <h3>10 — Subgrid</h3>
              <p>
                Subgrid settings control whether the model uses subgrid tables and how they are created or supplied. This is a powerful feature, but it should not expose fake options.
              </p>
              <div class="manual-field-table-wrap">
                <table class="manual-field-table">
                  <thead>
                    <tr>
                      <th>Option</th>
                      <th>Meaning</th>
                      <th>Current caution</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody>
                    <tr>
                      <td><span class="manual-inline-code">hydromt_generate</span></td>
                      <td>HydroMT/backend generates the subgrid data.</td>
                      <td>This is the normal Manual-style path when supported by the setup.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td><span class="manual-inline-code">premade_sbgfile</span></td>
                      <td>Use an existing subgrid file.</td>
                      <td>Only keep this visible if backend copying/linking and <span class="manual-inline-code">sfincs.inp</span> pointer support are real.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td><span class="manual-inline-code">none</span></td>
                      <td>Do not use subgrid.</td>
                      <td>Only valid for model configurations intended to run without subgrid.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Subgrid river/source settings</td>
                      <td>Control optional river-related subgrid setup.</td>
                      <td>Enabled options should require valid source/path settings.</td>
                    </tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="manual-callout">
              <p>
                <strong>Implementation note:</strong> pre-made subgrid support should be hidden or disabled unless the backend really copies/links the file into the run folder, points <span class="manual-inline-code">sfincs.inp</span> to it, and skips conflicting generation.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="manual-guide-panel" data-manual-guide-panel="forcing">
            <div class="manual-guide-card">
              <h3>11 — Forcing</h3>
              <p>
                Forcing describes what drives the model during the event: rainfall, water levels, discharge, wind, pressure, or other time-dependent inputs.
              </p>
              <div class="manual-field-table-wrap">
                <table class="manual-field-table">
                  <thead>
                    <tr>
                      <th>Forcing family</th>
                      <th>What it does</th>
                      <th>Warning</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Rainfall</td>
                      <td>Adds precipitation, either uniform, catalog/event-based, CSV/NetCDF, or another supported source.</td>
                      <td>Do not enable rainfall with a blank/invalid path unless using a valid uniform value.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Water-level boundary</td>
                      <td>Controls boundary water-level forcing.</td>
                      <td>Boundary time series must match the boundary geometry and model time window.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Discharge / source flow</td>
                      <td>Adds source or river discharge time series.</td>
                      <td>CSV discharge usually needs both point metadata and time-series CSV paths.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Wind / pressure</td>
                      <td>Adds atmospheric forcing when enabled.</td>
                      <td>Do not enable without valid source data and backend support.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Native SFINCS forcing</td>
                      <td>Uses files like <span class="manual-inline-code">sfincs.bzs</span>, <span class="manual-inline-code">sfincs.dis</span>, or <span class="manual-inline-code">precip_2d.nc</span>.</td>
                      <td>This usually belongs in Override Mode unless Manual backend support is explicitly confirmed.</td>
                    </tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="manual-callout">
              <p>
                <strong>Common mistake:</strong> matching the model time to the catalog runtime window does not prove every forcing file has full timestamp coverage. That deeper check should be added later.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="manual-guide-panel" data-manual-guide-panel="infiltration">
            <div class="manual-guide-card">
              <h3>12 — Infiltration</h3>
              <p>
                Infiltration controls how rainfall becomes runoff or is absorbed/stored. The correct settings depend on the selected method and available datasets.
              </p>
              <div class="manual-field-table-wrap">
                <table class="manual-field-table">
                  <thead>
                    <tr>
                      <th>Method / input</th>
                      <th>What it represents</th>
                      <th>Warning</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody>
                    <tr>
                      <td>SCS / Curve Number</td>
                      <td>Uses curve-number style runoff/infiltration behavior.</td>
                      <td>Needs valid curve number or SCS inputs.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Constant infiltration</td>
                      <td>Uses a constant or spatial input such as <span class="manual-inline-code">qinf</span>.</td>
                      <td>Units and spatial coverage must make sense.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Green-Ampt / Horton families</td>
                      <td>Use soil/hydraulic parameters such as conductivity, suction, or decay terms.</td>
                      <td>Do not enable parameter families without the required files/fields.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Initial moisture/storage fields</td>
                      <td>Can represent initial condition or event-dependent storage.</td>
                      <td>Some fields may be static, while others can be event-specific.</td>
                    </tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="manual-guide-panel" data-manual-guide-panel="output">
            <div class="manual-guide-card">
              <h3>13 — Output</h3>
              <p>
                Output settings control what the model writes and how often it writes it. These choices affect file size, postprocessing speed, and what can be validated later.
              </p>
              <div class="manual-field-table-wrap">
                <table class="manual-field-table">
                  <thead>
                    <tr>
                      <th>Output control</th>
                      <th>What it does</th>
                      <th>Warning</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Map output interval</td>
                      <td>Controls full-grid output frequency.</td>
                      <td>Frequent full-grid output can produce large NetCDF files.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>History output interval</td>
                      <td>Controls gauge/point/line time series output.</td>
                      <td>Useful for validation, usually cheaper than full maps.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Restart output</td>
                      <td>Writes restart files for continuing runs.</td>
                      <td>Only enable when restart workflow is needed.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Observation paths</td>
                      <td>Defines where history output is requested.</td>
                      <td>Enabled obs output with blank obs paths should warn.</td>
                    </tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="manual-guide-panel" data-manual-guide-panel="advanced">
            <div class="manual-guide-card">
              <h3>14 — Advanced</h3>
              <p>
                Advanced settings are for keys that are not represented by ordinary form fields. Use this section only for deliberate backend or SFINCS settings you understand.
              </p>
              <div class="manual-field-table-wrap">
                <table class="manual-field-table">
                  <thead>
                    <tr>
                      <th>Control</th>
                      <th>What it means</th>
                      <th>Warning</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Advanced JSON / text areas</td>
                      <td>Raw settings that get merged into the config.</td>
                      <td>Invalid JSON, wrong types, or stale keys can break a run.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Unknown key warnings</td>
                      <td>Warns when the config contains fields the schema/backend may not recognize.</td>
                      <td>Useful when loading older configs.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Strict schema validation</td>
                      <td>Can make unknown or wrong-type fields fail harder.</td>
                      <td>Only meaningful if backend/schema support is actually wired.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Preserved extra keys</td>
                      <td>Loaded config keys not visible on the page may be kept.</td>
                      <td>Manual should strip Override/native metadata so modes do not contaminate each other.</td>
                    </tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="manual-guide-panel" data-manual-guide-panel="postprocess">
            <div class="manual-guide-card">
              <h3>15 — Postprocess</h3>
              <p>
                Postprocessing turns SFINCS outputs into human-readable products such as maps, quicklooks, hydrographs, and summary reports.
              </p>
              <div class="manual-field-table-wrap">
                <table class="manual-field-table">
                  <thead>
                    <tr>
                      <th>Control</th>
                      <th>What it does</th>
                      <th>Warning</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Depth / velocity maps</td>
                      <td>Creates map-style outputs from model results.</td>
                      <td>Large domains can make map generation memory-heavy.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Hydrographs</td>
                      <td>Plots history output at observation points or lines.</td>
                      <td>Requires history output and observation definitions.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Basemap / overlay options</td>
                      <td>Adds map context when enabled.</td>
                      <td>Visual settings should not hide the actual data layer.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Postprocess resources</td>
                      <td>Controls the postprocess Slurm stage.</td>
                      <td>Increase memory/time for large full-domain outputs.</td>
                    </tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="manual-guide-panel" data-manual-guide-panel="safety_debug">
            <div class="manual-guide-card">
              <h3>16 — Safety / debug</h3>
              <p>
                This tab is for guardrails and diagnostics. It should help prevent accidental overwrites, unsafe project writes, stale paths, and hard-to-debug runs.
              </p>
              <div class="manual-field-table-wrap">
                <table class="manual-field-table">
                  <thead>
                    <tr>
                      <th>Control</th>
                      <th>What it does</th>
                      <th>Recommended default</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody>
                    <tr>
                      <td><span class="manual-inline-code">overwrite_existing_run</span></td>
                      <td>Allows existing run folders to be overwritten.</td>
                      <td>False unless intentionally replacing a scaffold/run.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td><span class="manual-inline-code">allow_writes_inside_proj</span></td>
                      <td>Allows writes inside shared project areas.</td>
                      <td>Usually false unless a specific workflow requires it.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>HydroMT file logging</td>
                      <td>Writes HydroMT logs to files.</td>
                      <td>Keep off by default because it previously caused major slowdown.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Timing diagnostics</td>
                      <td>Prints extra timing information during preprocessing.</td>
                      <td>Only keep visible if backend support is wired.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Debug warnings</td>
                      <td>Warn about missing paths, suspicious settings, or stale loaded-config keys.</td>
                      <td>Warnings should be read, not blindly ignored.</td>
                    </tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="manual-guide-panel" data-manual-guide-panel="backend_paths">
            <div class="manual-guide-card">
              <h3>17 — Backend paths</h3>
              <p>
                Backend paths tell the launcher which Python environment, scripts, and SFINCS container to use. Most users should use the current shared pipeline bundle paths.
              </p>
              <div class="manual-field-table-wrap">
                <table class="manual-field-table">
                  <thead>
                    <tr>
                      <th>Path</th>
                      <th>What it points to</th>
                      <th>Warning</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody>
                    <tr>
                      <td><span class="manual-inline-code">conda_python</span></td>
                      <td>The Python used to run backend pipeline stages.</td>
                      <td>The shared <span class="manual-inline-code">/proj</span> backend env is the normal default.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td><span class="manual-inline-code">conda_env_path</span></td>
                      <td>The environment folder for backend execution.</td>
                      <td>Old personal <span class="manual-inline-code">/users/e/p/epsilon</span> paths should be treated as fallback/dev paths.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td><span class="manual-inline-code">sfincs_container_path</span></td>
                      <td>The Apptainer/Singularity SFINCS container.</td>
                      <td>Blank or missing container paths should fail backend run requests.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Stage script paths</td>
                      <td>Preprocess and postprocess Python scripts.</td>
                      <td>Do not point to stale personal copies unless deliberately testing.</td>
                    </tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="manual-callout">
              <p>
                <strong>Future cleanup:</strong> backend path defaults should come from the launcher defaults/settings system, not hard-coded HTML values.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="manual-guide-panel" data-manual-guide-panel="check_all">
            <div class="manual-guide-card">
              <h3>Check all</h3>
              <p>
                Check All is the page-level review step. It should catch obvious missing paths, invalid settings, unsafe overwrite choices, timing problems, and mismatched Manual/Override behavior before backend preflight.
              </p>
              <div class="manual-field-table-wrap">
                <table class="manual-field-table">
                  <thead>
                    <tr>
                      <th>Check</th>
                      <th>What it means</th>
                      <th>How to treat it</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Runtime-window review</td>
                      <td>Compares requested model times to selected catalog runtime metadata.</td>
                      <td>A red mismatch should be fixed before running.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Local page checks</td>
                      <td>Checks visible and hidden page settings for obvious problems.</td>
                      <td>Use this before backend preflight.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Warnings</td>
                      <td>Suspicious but not always fatal choices.</td>
                      <td>Read and understand them. Some are okay; some are early signs of a bad run.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Errors</td>
                      <td>Settings that should block the workflow.</td>
                      <td>Fix errors before preflight/build/submit.</td>
                    </tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="manual-guide-panel" data-manual-guide-panel="submit">
            <div class="manual-guide-card">
              <h3>Submit</h3>
              <p>
                The Submit area turns the current page setup into backend actions. Treat Preflight, Build Scripts, and Submit as separate steps.
              </p>
              <div class="manual-field-table-wrap">
                <table class="manual-field-table">
                  <thead>
                    <tr>
                      <th>Button / area</th>
                      <th>What it does</th>
                      <th>What it does not mean</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Save Progress</td>
                      <td>Saves the current config for later reuse or review.</td>
                      <td>Not required before a normal one-off preflight.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Load Config</td>
                      <td>Loads a saved JSON setup back into the page.</td>
                      <td>Should not let Override/native metadata contaminate Manual Mode.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Run backend preflight</td>
                      <td>Validates the current setup and records a config/scaffold for the workflow.</td>
                      <td>Passing preflight does not mean SFINCS has run.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Build Slurm scripts</td>
                      <td>Creates the run-folder scaffold and Slurm scripts.</td>
                      <td>A scaffold is not completed model output.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Submit Slurm chain</td>
                      <td>Submits preprocessing, SFINCS, and postprocessing jobs.</td>
                      <td>Only use after config, checks, folder, and resources look correct.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td>Runner output box</td>
                      <td>Shows backend stdout/stderr and top success/failure messages.</td>
                      <td>Read failures carefully; the top message is only the summary.</td>
                    </tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="manual-callout">
              <p>
                <strong>Important:</strong> Build Scripts or Submit can fail after preflight passes when the destination run folder already contains real artifacts. That is a safety feature, not necessarily a bug.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

