        <div class="guide-card">
          <h3>About <span class="inline-code">sfincs.inp</span> in Override Mode</h3>
          <p>
            <span class="inline-code">sfincs.inp</span> is different from the other native files because it controls the run settings and points to the other input files. In Override Mode, the safer pattern is usually to let the launcher generate a fresh <span class="inline-code">sfincs.inp</span> from the page settings, while using detected native files for the model inputs.
          </p>
          <p>
            You may still use a detected <span class="inline-code">sfincs.inp</span> as a reference for timing or settings. Directly overriding <span class="inline-code">sfincs.inp</span> should be treated as an intentional reproduction choice, not the default beginner path.
          </p>
    
          <div class="callout">
            <p>
              <strong>Why the settings stay visible:</strong> even when <span class="inline-code">sfincs.inp</span> is selected or parsed, the model settings and Advanced Config areas remain visible so you can inspect important values before running.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
    
        <div class="guide-card">
          <h3>Advanced Config</h3>
          <p>
            Advanced Config is a raw settings area for keys that are not represented by the normal page controls. Use it for deliberate SFINCS or backend settings that you understand, such as projection behavior, numerical controls, output options, or specialized forcing switches.
          </p>
          <p>
            The main rule is simple: if you are not sure what a key does, do not change it here. A typo, wrong value type, missing comma, or outdated setting can change the run or make it fail before SFINCS starts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <figure class="guide-image">

          <img src="static/guide_images/override_04_advanced_config.png" alt="Image 4 — Advanced Config and model settings">

          <figcaption>

            <strong>Image 4 — Advanced Config and model settings.</strong> Model settings and Advanced Config stay visible so important SFINCS settings can still be checked before running.

          </figcaption>

        </figure>
    
        <div class="guide-card">
          <h3>Review checks</h3>
          <p>
            The review section is the “slow down and check yourself” part of the workflow. It should summarize the run name, output folder, selected static/event folders, detected files, selected overrides, timing, resources, overwrite behavior, and any warnings.
          </p>
          <p>
            These warnings are general checks. They do not mean every run is wrong, but they point to things that deserve attention before you submit. Missing file pointers, suspicious empty fields, invalid JSON, overwrite warnings, and mismatched static/event choices should not be ignored.
          </p>
        
          <div class="guide-table-wrap">
            <table class="guide-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Review item</th>
                  <th>What it is checking</th>
                  <th>Why it matters</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>Run name and output folder</td>
                  <td>Where the run will be written.</td>
                  <td>Prevents accidentally overwriting or confusing runs.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Static/event folder choices</td>
                  <td>Which native files are being pulled from which folders.</td>
                  <td>Prevents mixing the wrong physical setup with the wrong event.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Selected override files</td>
                  <td>Which detected files are actually active overrides.</td>
                  <td>Detection alone does not mean a file is being used.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Timing fields</td>
                  <td>Reference time, start time, stop time, and output intervals.</td>
                  <td>Bad timing can make forcing files misalign or shorten the run accidentally.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Advanced Config</td>
                  <td>Raw advanced settings and JSON syntax.</td>
                  <td>A typo or invalid value can break the run or change model behavior.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>File-pointer checks</td>
                  <td>Whether files referenced by <code>sfincs.inp</code> will exist in the run folder.</td>
                  <td>SFINCS should not be submitted with missing declared inputs.</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>
        
        <div class="guide-card">
          <h3>Preflight, build scripts, and submit</h3>
          <p>

            The submit section turns the reviewed page settings into backend actions. Treat Preflight, Build Scripts, and Submit as separate steps, not one giant button.

          </p>

          <p>

            Run backend preflight first. Preflight validates the current setup and records the config/scaffold needed by the run workflow. Build Scripts then prepares the Slurm scripts. Submit launches the Slurm chain.

          </p>
        
          <div class="guide-table-wrap">
            <table class="guide-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Button / action</th>
                  <th>What it does</th>
                  <th>What to check</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>

                  <td>Preflight</td>

                  <td>Runs backend validation and records the current page setup for the run workflow.</td>

                  <td>Fix errors before moving on. Warnings should be read, not skipped blindly.</td>

                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Build scripts</td>
                  <td>Creates the run-folder scaffold and Slurm scripts.</td>
                  <td>A scaffold is normal. It is not completed model output.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Submit</td>
                  <td>Submits the Slurm workflow for preprocessing, SFINCS, and postprocessing.</td>
                  <td>Only submit after the config, review checks, and output folder look correct.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Check status/logs</td>
                  <td>Looks at job status, launcher logs, Slurm logs, and model outputs.</td>
                  <td>If a job fails, logs are usually the fastest way to identify the failed stage.</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        
          <div class="callout">
            <p>
              <strong>Scaffold vs. real output:</strong> after Build Scripts, it is normal to see folders like scripts, logs, model, and postprocess. That does not mean the model has run yet. Real outputs appear only after the submitted jobs complete.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <figure class="guide-image">

          <img src="static/guide_images/override_05_review_submit.png" alt="Image 5 — Final review before submit">

          <figcaption>

            <strong>Image 5 — Final review before submit.</strong> Final review and submit area. The basic order is to read warnings, run preflight, build scripts, and then submit.

          </figcaption>

        </figure>
    
        
      </div>
    </section>
    
    
